 "summary": "Gemma is by far the most crowded category in the DEV.to Hacktoberfest Week 1 \"Touch Grass\" challenge. Several $200 featured categories are almost empty, and the three with the best payoff for one day of work are Tinker, DigitalOcean and Arduino. On 2026-10-10 the Touch Grass entries since Oct 5 numbered somewhere between about 700 and 1,160. About 16% of them name Gemma (186 of 1,161). TabPFN is a distant second at 17. Every other sponsor appears in 0 to 6 titles, descriptions or tags. Arduino has 0 but needs the physical board. DigitalOcean has 1 and Tinker has 2, and neither needs hardware you own. One project can enter every category it really uses but can win only once. The best plan is therefore to aim at one nearly empty $200 featured category and add a few $100 partner categories that cost almost nothing to claim. The recommended stack is Tinker (featured, $200) as the main target, plus Temporal and Entire ($100 partner categories, about 2 mentions each). Official starter code or a single CLI install makes each feasible in about a day. The suggested build is a durable outdoor \"go-window\" agent on a small open-weight model, fine-tuned through Tinker. Its write-up compares base and fine-tuned models on a set of held-out examples to show the measurable gain the Tinker category requires. None of the trending outdoor topics (bird migration, foliage, frost) were verified by this research, so treat the theme choice as a hypothesis. TabPFN entries already include a frost-data project and a leaf-peeping project, which is one more reason to avoid TabPFN.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "claim": "The Touch Grass field is large and still growing quickly. The #hf26challenge tag held about 2,172 unique articles on 2026-10-10, but it mixes in the earlier Build for a Friend weekend challenge (about 1,417 unique #weekendchallenge posts). Touch Grass posts dated Oct 5 or later without #weekendchallenge numbered about 699 in the first snapshot, and the verifier estimated 670-690 genuinely on-theme. A later re-pull the same day found 1,161 posts dated Oct 5 or later. New posts were arriving at roughly 125-200 a day (Oct 9: 203), and the deadline is Oct 11, 11:59 PM PDT, so expect a last-day surge.",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources": [
    "https://dev.to/api/articles?tag=hf26challenge&per_page=1000",
    "https://dev.to/t/hf26challenge"
   ],
   "evidence": "Primary dev.to API, pulled independently by the claimant and the verifier (vote 3-0). Daily counts: 10-05 43, 10-06 147, 10-07 140, 10-08 125, 10-09 203, 10-10 41 (partial day). The challenge page itself shows no entry counts, only a View Entries link, so the tag feed is the only way to measure saturation. The tag feed also includes non-entries, such as the winners announcement.",
   "vote": "3-0"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Ranked table of categories by estimated competition. The figure is sponsor-name mentions in the title, description or tags of posts dated Oct 5 or later, from the verifier's re-pull of 1,161 posts. LEAST crowded first: Arduino 0 (featured $200, needs hardware) | DigitalOcean 1 (featured $200) | SerpApi 1 ($100) | Mastra 1 ($100) | Tinker 2 (featured $200) | Temporal 2 ($100) | Backboard 2 ($100) | Sentry 2 ($100) | Entire 2 ($100) | Tiger Data 3 ($100) | MongoDB 3 ($100) | Render 5 (featured $200) | GitHub Copilot 5 ($100) | ElevenLabs 6 ($100) | TabPFN 17 (featured $200) | Gemma 186 (featured $200). Weighing prize against competition, the best targets are the near-empty featured categories: Arduino if you have the board, otherwise Tinker or DigitalOcean.",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "sources": [
    "https://dev.to/api/articles?tag=hf26challenge&per_page=1000",
    "https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week1-2026-10-05"
   ],
   "evidence": "The verifier reproduced the counts (vote 3-0), but they come from keyword matching on title, description and tags only. They are not the Prize Categories sections inside post bodies, so they are a stand-in for real entries. The earlier 699-post snapshot gave Gemma 119, TabPFN 14 and most others 1. An earlier claim that Entire, Tiger Data, Arduino and Copilot had zero mentions was refuted 1-2, because Entire and Tiger each later showed 2-3. Real per-category counts are probably higher once post bodies are read, but the relative order is the useful signal.",
   "vote": "3-0 (ranking); the zero-mention variant was refuted 1-2"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Avoid entering Gemma as a prize category. TabPFN is the second most contested category and already has on-theme entries such as frost forecasting and leaf peeping. You can still use Gemma as your open model without entering the Gemma category.",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources": [
    "https://dev.to/api/articles?tag=hf26challenge&per_page=1000"
   ],
   "evidence": "Gemma appears in about 16% of Touch Grass posts, roughly 11 times as often as TabPFN. The gemma tag alone appears 127 times across the whole tag. Spot checks of the feed found 'PLOT - TabPFN on Real Frost Data', 'Leaf Peep' and 'Lake Lookout ... (Gemma 4, fully offline)', so foliage, frost and offline-Gemma ideas are already taken.",
   "vote": "3-0"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Past results show that no category is ever left with zero entries. In the previous HF26 challenge, Build for a Friend (Oct 2-5, with the same 16 categories and prize amounts), every category had a winner, including Tinker, Entire, Backboard, Tiger Data, Temporal and Arduino. That challenge also set a participation record, nearly triple the previous one. Low-competition categories still get a few entries, so the quality of your entry matters.",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources": [
    "https://dev.to/devteam/congrats-to-the-hacktoberfest-weekend-challenge-build-for-a-friend-winners-pgc",
    "https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-weekend-2026-10-01"
   ],
   "evidence": "The DEV team winners post (Oct 9) names a winner for each of the 16 categories. Examples: Tinker went to Rakshak, a scam-checker; Entire to Viva; Tiger Data to Priyanshu Jha; Arduino to Project JARVIS; Temporal to PaidYet. The post says the challenge was 'nearly tripling our previous record for submissions' but gives no counts per category. The themes and lengths differ (a 3-day sprint then, about 6 days now), so the comparison is close but not exact.",
   "vote": "3-0"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Under the rules and prize structure, stacking categories raises your odds, not your payout. One project can enter every category it really uses, but each person can win only once per challenge. Prizes are $250 plus DEV++ for the overall winner, $200 for each featured category (Render, TabPFN, Tinker, Arduino, DigitalOcean, Gemma) and $100 for each partner category (Backboard, ElevenLabs, Entire, GitHub Copilot, Mastra, MongoDB Atlas, Sentry, SerpApi, Temporal, Tiger Data). Writing quality carries the most weight in judging.",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources": [
    "https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week1-2026-10-05"
   ],
   "evidence": "From the official FAQ: 'One project can enter as many categories as it genuinely uses... you can win once per challenge.' Entrants list categories in the Prize Categories section of their post. You may publish one submission per challenge, it must be in English, and teams can have up to 4 people.",
   "vote": "3-0"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Tinker (featured, $200, about 2 competing mentions) can be done in one day without your own GPU. It is a cloud training API: you write the LoRA training loop on a CPU machine and Tinker runs the GPU compute, for models from 1B to over 1T parameters (dense and MoE, text and vision, 28+ models including Llama, Qwen, DeepSeek-V3.1, GPT-OSS and Kimi K2). Tinker is now generally available with no waitlist. The category requires a measurable gain over a baseline, so you need an evaluation set.",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "sources": [
    "https://tinker-docs.thinkingmachines.ai",
    "https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/tinker-general-availability/"
   ],
   "evidence": "The docs say: 'Write training loops locally � Tinker handles the GPUs' and 'Author code on a CPU machine.' The GA blog post confirms open access. The model-range claim passed 2-1, and '28+ models' is a headline number nobody checked against the model list. Pricing and credits were NOT verified: a third-party site reports $150 in promotional credits, which is unconfirmed. Fine-tuning plus evaluation also adds run time.",
   "vote": "3-0 (feasibility), 2-1 (model range)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Entire ($100, about 2 mentions) costs almost nothing to add to any project. Its CLI is open source under the MIT license and records sessions from Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Copilot CLI, OpenCode and other agents. Each agent commit becomes a checkpoint that pairs the code change with the prompt, reasoning and tool calls behind it. That is exactly what the category asks for: 'Share the agent sessions behind your project in the write-up, or search past agent work to explain why code exists.'",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources": [
    "https://docs.entire.io/cli/overview",
    "https://github.com/entireio/cli",
    "https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week1-2026-10-05"
   ],
   "evidence": "Confirmed by the docs and by the MIT license in the GitHub repo. Install and enable the CLI in the repo before you start building, because commits are not captured on their own. Sharing and searching happen on entire.io. Pi support is in preview, Copilot is supported in the CLI only, and entire review and entire blame are experimental.",
   "vote": "3-0"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Temporal ($100, about 2 mentions) has official starter code for durable AI agents, and that code can be pointed at an open-weight model. The durable-ai-agent tutorial (Python SDK) runs multi-turn conversations as Workflows and tool and LLM calls as Activities with retries. The AI Cookbook adds 16 recipes, including 'Hello world with LiteLLM.' Both call the model through LiteLLM, so changing LLM_MODEL and LLM_BASE_URL to a local Ollama or vLLM endpoint should satisfy the open-source AI requirement.",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources": [
    "https://learn.temporal.io/tutorials/ai/durable-ai-agent/",
    "https://docs.temporal.io/ai-cookbook"
   ],
   "evidence": "Official docs. The defaults are OpenAI gpt-4o and Gemini, so they do NOT qualify as-is. Switching to a local model is an inference: LiteLLM supports ollama/* model strings, but Temporal's docs never demonstrate it. The tutorial asks for JSON-only output through the prompt, and the verifier warned that small local models follow it less reliably. That weakness is a natural place to show a measurable Tinker gain.",
   "vote": "3-0"
  },
  {
   "claim": "TabPFN is the easiest sponsor to build with technically: a hosted tabpfn-client (pip install tabpfn-client, set TABPFN_TOKEN), a hosted remote MCP server at https://api.priorlabs.ai/mcp/server using OAuth and Streamable HTTP, zero-shot time-series forecasting (TabPFN-TS), and a free tier (by default 50M prediction tokens a day and 200M a month). But with about 17 mentions it is crowded by this challenge's standards, so only use it as a secondary category.",
   "confidence": "high",
   "sources": [
    "https://docs.priorlabs.ai/quickstart",
    "https://docs.priorlabs.ai/agentic/mcp",
    "https://docs.priorlabs.ai/agentic/mcp.md",
    "https://docs.priorlabs.ai/capabilities/forecasting",
    "https://docs.priorlabs.ai/api-reference/metering",
    "https://priorlabs.ai/pricing"
   ],
   "evidence": "You need a Prior Labs account. Running out of quota returns HTTP 429, and Thinking mode is limited to 20 fits a month. The docs disagree on the version name (3.5-Plus vs 3-Plus). TabPFN-TS models seasonality well but does not extrapolate trends, so seasonal migration counts suit it better than once-a-year foliage peaks. A claim that TabPFN-TS meets the open/local-model requirement by itself was refuted 0-3.",
   "vote": "3-0"
  },
  {
   "claim": "RECOMMENDED IDEA, a synthesis rather than a verified fact. 'Go-Window: the agent that wants you off your phone.' Each Friday a Temporal workflow gathers weather and other outdoor data for the user's area and produces ONE short, printable or lock-screen plan (for example 'Saturday 7-9am, Ridge Trail, low wind, migration night ahead'), then stays quiet until the next week. Its success metric is screen time avoided. The open model is a small open-weight model (for example a Llama 3.x or Qwen 1-8B variant on Tinker) LoRA-fine-tuned to turn raw conditions into a strict-JSON go/no-go decision plus a time window, served through LiteLLM. Categories to enter: Tinker (main target, $200), Temporal, Entire, and optionally Sentry Agent Tracing for latency, token and cost traces (about 2 mentions). Measurable gain for Tinker: about 300-500 synthetic or hand-labelled condition-to-decision examples split train and test. Report base vs LoRA on JSON-validity rate, go/no-go accuracy and time-window match. 24-hour plan: (1) about 1h: enable Entire, scaffold from the Temporal durable-agent tutorial or LiteLLM recipe; (2) about 3h: build the dataset and evaluation script and measure the baseline; (3) about 3h: train the LoRA on Tinker and re-evaluate; (4) about 4h: wire the Workflow (fetch data, decide, render one card) as Activities with retries, then demo by killing the worker mid-run and showing it resume; (5) about 2h: deploy and record a GIF; (6) about 4h: write it up. Write-up angle: a story ('my app's KPI is minutes you don't spend in it'), a baseline-vs-fine-tuned results table, the crash-and-resume demo, and links to the Entire checkpoints that explain design decisions. Writing quality carries the most judging weight.",
   "confidence": "low",
   "sources": [
    "https://tinker-docs.thinkingmachines.ai",
